%S A398149 1,2,2,4,1,5,3,7,1,2,2,4,6,1,5,3,1,4,13,6,1,1,1,1,6,1,2,1,2,3,1,2,1,2,
%T A398149 1,2,3,4,2,4,3,2,3,2,1,5,3,4,4,5,3,9,3,15,1,1,1,3,2,1,1,3,3,1,4,1,1,1,
%U A398149 3,3,7,4,3,2,1,2,3,1,4,1,4,1,6,3,2,5,6,5,3
%N A398149 a(1) = 1; for n > 1, a(n) is the smallest positive number on a square spiral such the resulting sums of the numbers in each row and column is distinct.
%C A398149 In the first ten million terms the lowest number not to appear is 153; it is conjectured all positive numbers eventually appear. In this range the largest value is a(6454127) = 248.
%H A398149 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A398149/b398149.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A398149 The spiral begins:
%e A398149                                 .
%e A398149                                 .
%e A398149     3---2---1---2---1---2---1   1
%e A398149     |                       |   |
%e A398149     4   1---3---5---1---6   3   1
%e A398149     |   |               |   |   |
%e A398149     2   4   1---4---2   4   2  15
%e A398149     |   |   |       |   |   |   |
%e A398149     4  13   5   1---2   2   1   3
%e A398149     |   |   |           |   |   |
%e A398149     3   6   3---7---1---2   2   9
%e A398149     |   |                   |   |
%e A398149     2   1---1---1---1---6---1   3
%e A398149     |                           |
%e A398149     3---2---1---5---3---4---4---5
%e A398149 .
%e A398149 a(2) = 2 as, after a(1) = 1, the resulting row and column sums are 1, 2 and 3.
%e A398149 a(3) = 2 as, after 1 and 2, the resulting row and column sums are 1, 2, 3 and 4.
%e A398149 a(8) = 7 as, before a(8) is found, the row and column sums are 3, 4, 5, 7, 8 and 9. As a(8) is in the row with a sum of 3 and a column sum of 5, the  next term cannot be 1, 2, 3 (as 5 can not become 8), 4, 5, or 6, as 3 plus those candidates would create a sum that already exists. This leaves 7 as the lowest available next term. The resulting row and column sums are then 4, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 12.
%Y A398149 Cf. A361724, A357991, A355270, A355271.
